# DuetSoftwareFramework packages from a separately published build of our
# own, installed in place of the Duet3D archive's.
#
# One line per package:
#
#   <package> sha256:<checksum of the published .deb> <https location>
#
# The DSF packages pin each other to one exact version and check it again at
# runtime, so a build of our own is installed as a whole: every package that
# pkg/build.sh --packages=progs,dwc,meta produces in the fork, at a version
# <dsf.version>+<suffix> (3.7.0~rc.2+mp.1 for a modified 3.7.0~rc.2).
# duetwebcontrol is among them: the build packages our fork of Duet Web
# Control, Meltingplot/DuetWebControl, at a version of the same form, and the
# meta package pins it. duetsd and reprapfirmware come from the archive, where
# the build's meta package pins them; reprapfirmware at dsf.rrf_version, which
# the meta package has to accept. An empty list installs the archive's
# packages. Only a published build is taken, checked against the pin: no local
# path, no fallback, a mismatch stops the build. See "Installing a
# DuetSoftwareFramework build of our own" in the README.
#
# A board firmware file is not a package of the build; it goes through
# firmware.list.

# 3.7.0-rc.2+mp.2: upstream v3.7-dev at 3.7.0-rc.2, which moves the
# accelerometers from boards[] to sensors.accelerometers, plus our fix for a
# DCS crash on a non-finite expression result, and Duet Web Control from our
# fork, 3.7.0-rc.2+mp.2, with the CHX 350 operator interface. Duet3D has
# published no 3.7.0-rc.2 yet; the meta package of this build still asks for
# reprapfirmware 3.7.0~rc.2 and cannot be installed with dsf.rrf_version
# 3.7.0~rc.1. Earlier builds: mp.9 of 3.7.0-rc.1 brought remote sensors on CAN
# expansion boards, mp.8 checked N line numbers in job and macro files, mp.7
# RRF-style checksums on numbered lines, mp.6 kept the object model in step
# after an SPI transfer timeout.
